\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Distribution of generated PaperBench-style tests across six rubric categories for adaptive pruning, BAM, and BBox. Tests marked with \texttt  {xfail} are counted as failures rather than being removed, while explicit \texttt  {pytest.skip} cases are reported separately. The figure shows that the generated suites are dominated by expected-failing rubric checks, especially in method, asset, and result-reproduction categories, which supports our claim that execution-grounded paper-to-code evaluation must distinguish ordinary passing tests from qualitative smoke checks and incomplete reproduction tests.\relax }}{16}{figure.caption.30}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:xfail-category-counts}{{5}{16}{Distribution of generated PaperBench-style tests across six rubric categories for adaptive pruning, BAM, and BBox. Tests marked with \texttt {xfail} are counted as failures rather than being removed, while explicit \texttt {pytest.skip} cases are reported separately. The figure shows that the generated suites are dominated by expected-failing rubric checks, especially in method, asset, and result-reproduction categories, which supports our claim that execution-grounded paper-to-code evaluation must distinguish ordinary passing tests from qualitative smoke checks and incomplete reproduction tests.\relax }{figure.caption.30}{}}
